If you bought an iPhone Duo on launch day and are having second thoughts about keeping Apple's first foldable iPhone, tomorrow could be your last chance to return it for a refund.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/iphone-duo-colors.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="899" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1063421" /><br/>
Apple officially launched the iPhone Duo on Friday, October 23, so the standard 14-day return window for customers who purchased and received the device that day expires on <strong>Friday, November 6</strong>.<br/>
<br/>
Under <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/help/returns_refund">Apple's U.S. return policy</a>, customers have 14 calendar days from receiving a product to return it, whether they purchased it online or at an Apple retail store. A similar standard return period <a href="https://www.apple.com/uk/shop/help/returns_refund">applies in the UK</a> and across much of the rest of Europe.<br/>
<br/>
If you bought the iPhone Duo in Canada, then you have slightly longer. Under <a href="https://www.apple.com/ca/shop/help/returns_refund">Apple's Canadian return policy</a>, buyers have 15 calendar days from receiving their device to initiate a return, so if you got your device on launch day then you have until <strong>Saturday, November 7</strong> to return it.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>UK and EU Online Customers Have More Time to Send It Back</h2><br/>
There's an important distinction to note if you're in the <a href="https://www.apple.com/uk/shop/help/rma_stepbystep#:~:text=You%20can%20indicate,for%20their%20collection.">UK</a> or the <a href="https://www.apple.com/ie/shop/open/salespolicies#:~:text=Returns-,You%20have%20the%20right,method%20within%2014%20days.,-Please">European Union</a> and you purchased your iPhone Duo online. Under consumer protection laws, you have 14 days from receiving the device to <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/eudr/2011/83/article/11#:~:text=Before%20the%20expiry,from%20the%20contract."><em>notify</em></a> Apple that you intend to return it. Once you've made that notification, you have a <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/eudr/2011/83/article/14#:~:text=Unless%20the%20trader,days%20has%20expired.">further 14 days</a> to send the device back or arrange a collection.<br/>
<br/>
In other words, if you're a UK or EU customer who received the iPhone Duo on October 23 and you request a return tomorrow, then you could have until <strong>November 20</strong> to dispatch it. This also applies to devices ordered online and collected at an Apple Store. However, if you actually purchased your device directly at a physical Apple Store – which probably means you waited in a separate line to collections – then you generally need to return it within the initial 14-day window.<br/>
<br/>
Note that the November 6 deadline doesn't apply to everyone who pre-ordered the iPhone Duo. Apple's return period is calculated from when customers received their devices, rather than when they placed their orders, so if your iPhone Duo arrived after launch day, you have more time to return it for a refund – if that's your preference.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>What to Know Before Returning Your iPhone Duo</h2><br/>
Apple asks customers to return their devices with the original packaging and included accessories. Also, remember to disable Find My for the iPhone Duo before returning the device. Returns can be initiated through Apple's online Order Status page or, in the US, at an Apple retail store.<br/>
<br/>
Keep in mind that these policies apply only to purchases made directly from Apple – if you bought an iPhone Duo through a carrier or third-party retailer, you'll be subject to that seller's return policy. <br/>
<br/>
